WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:02.700
Stanley Kubrick's The Shining is a masterpiece.

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:07.680
As a result, some details in the film that
seem out of place serve to illustrate the

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.020
supernatural forces at work in the Overlook.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.600
Other inconsistencies can't be dismissed so
easily.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.780
Here are a few dumb things in The Shining
that we just can't ignore.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.920
The creepy sets and confusing layout of the
Overlook hotel are a central part of the plot

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:23.540
of The Shining.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:28.660
The halls are a labyrinth, and the feeling
of being lost and isolated with no idea of

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.800
what's around the next corner is a key part
of what makes everything so scary.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:37.040
This mysterious feeling is mirrored by the
hedge maze outside, and while it's only fitting

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.540
that the climax of the film takes place there,
the location is established very early on.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.460
Just… not as early as it should've been.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:49.600
The opening scene of the film gives the audience
an aerial view of the Overlook and the grounds,

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.420
including the hotel itself, the surrounding
forests, and the mountains in the distance.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:58.300
The feeling of isolation is there, reinforcing
the idea that this is the kind of place where

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.200
no can hear you scream for help.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.180
Eagle-eyed viewers will notice that there's
something missing, though.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:07.500
The hedge maze is supposed to be right next
to the hotel, just a short walk away from

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.400
the entrance, but in the shot that establishes
our setting, it's nowhere to be found, despite

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.360
the fact that it's enormous, and would definitely
be visible from the sky.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.860
Maybe it just sprung up as a product of Jack's
increasing madness, or maybe the curse at

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.180
the heart of the Overlook just really likes
metaphors.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:29.540
Before officially accepting the caretaker
job, Jack goes to the Overlook for an interview.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:34.140
At first, it's pretty standard, with Mr. Ullman,
the manager, begins telling him all about

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.980
the job and his potential responsibilities.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:41.480
He warns him about just how far away from
civilization he'll really be, explaining there's

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:46.020
basically nothing to do outside of the hotel,
and that he and his family will be alone.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:51.280
He then drops what might be the biggest red
flag that any job interview has ever had while

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.180
discussing how the previous caretaker lost
the job.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.460
"Killed his family with an axe."

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.860
Ullman also mentions in passing that although
they're in Colorado, there's no skiing in

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:05.400
the area.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:09.700
Clearly, this is just to reinforce the idea
that there's no quick way off the mountain,

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.980
but it's contradicted by the film itself.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:16.280
It's tough to spot, but In those aerial shots
at the beginning of the film, you can see

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:19.660
a ski lift going up the side of a mountain
not far from the hotel.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:25.580
Before the events of the film take place,
Jack Torrance came home drunk one night and

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.220
dislocated Danny's shoulder.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.640
He then promised that he wouldn't touch alcohol
ever again.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:35.400
Wendy tells Danny's doctor about the incident,
saying that it's been five months since Danny's

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.520
injury, and so far, Jack has stayed true to
his word.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.090
Perhaps unsurprisingly, however, Jack later
makes a comment that doesn't match up with

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:43.740
Wendy's timeline.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.600
"I did hurt him once, okay?"

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:53.980
When he's sitting at the bar in the Gold Room
and griping to the bartender, Lloyd, about

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.900
what happened, he says that it all went down
three years ago.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.960
There are a couple of possible explanations
for this.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:04.440
The most obvious is that it's just a minor
mistake in the script, which makes sense given

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.800
how many drafts The Shining went through before
it was filmed.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:11.260
With Kubrick behind the camera, though, there's
also a chance that this was totally intentional.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:15.940
Maybe Jack is disconnecting from time as the
isolation of the Overlook drives him mad.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.920
Maybe he's remembering an incident from Grady's
life, not his own.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:25.080
Or maybe, in the most disturbing possibility,
that incident a few months ago wasn't the

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.720
only time that Jack hurt Danny, and he doesn't
even remember the more recent abuse.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:35.420
When Jack visits the Gold Room and meets Lloyd
the bartender, we're not sure if Lloyd is

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:38.560
a ghost or a projection of Jack's twisted
mind.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.920
Regardless, Jack strikes up a conversation
with him, and orders himself a bourbon on

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:43.480
the rocks.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:48.840
He drinks throughout his rant about how Wendy
won't forgive him for his abuse of Danny,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.900
and when Wendy actually arrives in the Gold
Room, crying about a mysterious woman who

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:58.340
tried to strangle Danny, Lloyd suddenly disappears,
along with all of the liquor and decor behind

00:03:58.340 --> 00:03:58.880
the bar.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:03.280
It's a genuinely unsettling scene, but either
someone in the props department got a little

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:07.889
mixed up, or Lloyd isn't actually the best
bartender from Timbuktu to Portland, Maine

00:04:07.889 --> 00:04:09.000
— or Portland, Oregon.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:14.620
Instead of the bourbon he asked for, Lloyd
pours Jack's drink from a bottle of Jack Daniel's

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:17.000
Old No. 7, which isn't quite what he asked
for.

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:21.720
While Jack Daniel's is legally classified
as straight bourbon, the distillery never

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.300
markets it or refers to it as anything but
whiskey.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:29.050
Lloyd's technically correct, but a hard drinker
like Jack Torrance would definitely know the

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:33.240
difference between a Tennessee Whiskey and
a Kentucky Bourbon, even in Colorado.

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:39.360
As the Torrance family drives to the Overlook,
we see that they're traveling in a small,

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.700
yellow Volkswagen Beetle.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:45.620
The shots from inside of the car reveal that
while it's fairly cramped space, there's at

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:47.600
least enough room for Danny to move around..

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:52.820
Later, in the lobby of the Overlook, however,
Jack gestures to the family's huge pile of

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:53.980
luggage sitting by the door.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:58.920
Jack, Wendy, and Danny are staying at the
Overlook for months and there won't be anywhere

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:01.520
for them to go shopping, so they definitely
need all their clothes.

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:06.172
If they were really making that journey in
a VW Beetle, though, there's no way that they

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:09.680
could've crammed their entire wardrobe in
the car and still had room to breathe.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.820
Even with the creepy, space-bending geography
of the Overlook, there's only so much that

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:17.840
a Volkswagen can hold… unless they also
bought their car from an ancient, cursed burial

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:18.380
ground.

